WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.405 --> 00:00:02.820
During this brutal
moment in "The Fanatic,"

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.960
John Travolta's fingers get shot off.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.730
This is what it looked
like in a test shoot

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:09.360
as the FX team was figuring out

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.720
how to make it work on camera.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.470
To create fake injuries
for movies and TV shows,

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:16.970
makeup artists need an
intimate understanding

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.100
of the human anatomy in order to, say,

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.690
create an exact replica
of Travolta's hand,

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.220
and they need to know
what looks best on camera,

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.680
like using pressurized air

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.360
to blow the fingers off that replica.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.826
Here's how seven gory
makeup effects are created.

00:00:33.657 --> 00:00:35.410
For this scene in "The Irishman,"

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:37.300
where Robert De Niro stomps on

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.530
and breaks a shopkeeper's hand,

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:41.877
SPFX had to go beyond looks

00:00:41.877 --> 00:00:45.080
and make something that would
also move realistically,

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.230
bending and breaking
in just the right way.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:49.847
It started with molding the arm

00:00:49.847 --> 00:00:52.530
in skin-like silicone mixed with plaster,

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:56.040
so the final piece was
somewhat translucent and rigid.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.380
But instead of filling it in,

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:00.050
the FX artists added a metal armature,

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:01.580
a frame similar to what you'd see

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.440
inside a stop-motion puppet.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.770
While the outer layer of
the arm provided rigidness,

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:09.370
this armature created more
bends in the hand and fingers.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:11.140
When filming, they placed the fake arm

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.020
in actor Marco Greco's sleeve

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.750
and carefully positioned the camera

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.510
so that his body blocked his real arm.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.300
The result was almost too effective.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.610
According to SPFX's Brett Schmidt,

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:23.760
during one of the takes,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.630
one of the fingers broke and spun around.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.110
Exactly the kind of disturbing movie magic

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.527
they were hoping for.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.460
In the 2019 film "The Fanatic,"

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.970
John Travolta's character
gets shot in the hand

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:38.690
and loses his fingers.

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:39.803
It's a gruesome moment

00:01:39.803 --> 00:01:42.580
that primarily relied
on practical effects.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:45.760
Special makeup effects artist
Mike Marino and his team

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.460
had to construct a
realistic-looking prosthetic hand

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:50.222
with detachable fingers.

00:01:50.222 --> 00:01:53.300
They create the base like
any other prosthetic piece.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.930
First, they cast it in silicone

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.630
for skin-like translucency,

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:59.110
with hairs punched in for realism.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.420
They created the hand
and forearm together,

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.310
but they needed to attach
the fingers separately.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.710
They rigged the fake
hand to a series of tubes

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:08.130
and pressurized air,

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.650
allowing the fingers to blow off on cue

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:14.040
and blood to spurt out of
the wounds at the same time.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.690
To hide the apparatus from viewers,

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:19.040
they used a clever camera
angle that you can see here.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:20.340
Watching the finished scene,

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.815
you'd never know the hand
wasn't attached to anything.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:28.460
In the HBO series "I
Know This Much Is True,"

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:31.800
Mark Ruffalo's character
cuts off his own hand.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.150
His resulting injury is
frequently visible on camera,

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.330
but creating the illusion
of a missing limb

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.520
requires not only digital effects,

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.660
but also careful attention
to practical details.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.490
According to ProRen's Mike Marino,

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.130
a green glove would make his hand

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.150
look too long and unnatural.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.610
So instead, Marino painted Ruffalo's hand

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.440
a specific shade of green.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.720
This allowed FuseFX to digitally erase

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.650
his real hand from the shots.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:57.897
With his hand gone,

00:02:57.897 --> 00:02:59.913
Ruffalo also wore a prosthetic piece

00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:02.088
to further emphasize the injury.

00:03:02.088 --> 00:03:03.440
Marino made two designs

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.740
depending on which one the scene dictated:

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.300
a fresh, bloodier wound

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.350
and a cleaner, sewn-up wound.

00:03:11.002 --> 00:03:14.130
In season two of "Westworld,"
actress Rinko Kikuchi

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:17.050
splits the Shogun's head in half.

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:20.415
But since the Shogun's body
continues to squirm around,

00:03:20.415 --> 00:03:22.477
the team couldn't just
make a prosthetic head.

00:03:22.477 --> 00:03:25.110
They had to consider the entire body.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.480
Justin Raleigh and his
team at Fractured FX

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.018
also had to create this injury

00:03:29.018 --> 00:03:31.500
with several different
camera angles in mind.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.530
They first took a silicone
life cast of the actor's head

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.220
to capture every physical bit of his face.

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.160
They then made a replica out of clay,

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.973
which allowed them to sculpt details

00:03:40.973 --> 00:03:42.830
before casting it in more silicone

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:44.600
and then punching in all the character's

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:46.470
head and facial hair.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.820
Unlike other decapitated
heads on the show,

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:50.520
this one was split through the cheek,

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.240
meaning Raleigh had to
construct a tongue, esophagus,

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:56.210
and the surrounding muscle
and tissue inside the head.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.510
But the trickiest part was this moment,

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.880
when the Shogun takes a few
steps before collapsing.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:03.760
So the fake head was actually created

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.170
so it could be worn by a stunt performer,

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:07.518
and that blue hood he's wearing

00:04:07.518 --> 00:04:09.657
allowed the VFX team to erase him,

00:04:09.657 --> 00:04:12.773
so his real head wouldn't
pop up on camera.

00:04:14.645 --> 00:04:18.090
Burn wounds are a common
injury in TV shows and movies,

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:19.730
but making them look realistic

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:22.990
involves a close attention
to texture and color.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:25.760
So special makeup effects
artist Ben Bornstein

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.360
turns to a surprising reference:

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.060
burned chicken.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.220
This is particularly
helpful in picking out

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.340
the color palette for fresh burns.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.420
Heavy black in the deepest
part of the wounds,

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.010
shades of red, and lots of yellow.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:42.010
Besides heavy prosthetics,
Bornstein can also create

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:45.500
convincing burns with much
less time-consuming methods,

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.320
like 3D-printed wounds
for background characters

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.170
or bald-cap vinyl pieces

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:52.210
that have actually been set on fire.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:53.420
Some of these pieces are filled

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:55.230
with KY Jelly to create depth

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.290
and are then blended onto a painted spot

00:04:57.290 --> 00:04:58.860
on a performer's arm.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.480
In the end, the most
convincing form of this injury

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:03.860
is when all of these
methods are blended together

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:06.009
and you can't tell which is which.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.832
In 1990's "The Grifters," John Cusack

00:05:10.832 --> 00:05:13.370
gets slashed in the throat with glass.

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:16.190
This wound required a fake neck piece,

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:17.898
but back then, prosthetic pieces

00:05:17.898 --> 00:05:20.180
were mostly made with foam latex,

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:21.260
which would have soaked up

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:23.310
all the fake blood used in the scene.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.680
To make this piece last
through multiple takes,

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.050
StudioADI's Tom Woodruff
Jr. and Alec Gillis

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:32.800
constructed a fake neck out
of much sturdier gelatin.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:34.690
Woodruff and Gillis then made an incision

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:36.320
in the appliance right here.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:37.700
That would be the jugular vein,

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:38.910
which, according to Woodruff,

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:42.010
would make the character
realistically bleed the most.

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:44.220
They loaded blood into a large syringe

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.590
that was connected to a flexible
tube under Cusack's outfit,

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:50.050
which connected to a
flat tube under the neck.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:52.570
When he got hit with the
briefcase and the glass shattered,

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:55.800
they pushed down on the syringe
and released the fake blood.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.680
So it looks like the glass he's holding

00:05:57.680 --> 00:05:59.305
is what really cuts him.

00:06:01.039 --> 00:06:03.480
The operation scenes
Justin Raleigh worked on

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:04.970
in "Westworld" and "The Knick"

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:07.600
went far beyond squirts of blood.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.472
Crafting realistic surgery wounds

00:06:09.472 --> 00:06:11.190
involves thinking backwards,

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:14.860
down through the different
layers of skin, fat, and muscle.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:17.090
When creating an original negative mold,

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:18.500
Raleigh works in reverse

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:20.950
to control the thickness of each layer.

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:22.780
From reading anatomy textbooks,

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:24.750
Raleigh realized that Thandiwe Newton's

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:26.860
inner skin layer wouldn't be bare,

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.720
but instead covered in a layer of fat.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:32.360
Blending the pieces perfectly
into Newton's real skin

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:35.700
made it look like the wound
was actually part of her body.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.020
But he did have to dial some details back.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.700
By holding back on fake blood,

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:41.300
all of those muscles and vessels

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:45.260
specific to a human neck
looked much clearer on camera.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:48.170
All of the deep research,
endless experimentation,

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:50.500
and knowledge of what looks good on camera

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:54.070
is what takes gore makeup
from good to great.

00:06:54.070 --> 00:06:55.960
So the next time you see blood squirting

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:57.490
out of an actor's body,

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:59.400
remember that there was a whole crew

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.400
making sure they hit the right vein.

